schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: "Commons governance principles (Ostrom) apply to source-system stewardship"
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    A source system that no one is allowed to govern eventually gets trashed
    or abandoned; Ostrom's commons-design principles — clearly defined
    boundaries (who may use/change the resource) and graduated sanctions
    (proportionate responses to rule-breaking) — translate directly into
    "who can edit/add/delete" and "how to handle a contributor who keeps
    adding unsourced claims."
  evidence: >-
    Cites Elinor Ostrom's Nobel-winning work on common-pool resource
    governance (P2P Foundation summary of her eight design principles) and
    the Token Engineering Commons' "Cultural Build," which explicitly builds
    on those same eight principles.
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/source-systems.md ([P2P], [TEC] references)
  interpretation: >-
    Frames source-system stewardship as fundamentally a social/governance
    problem, not a tooling problem — "tools store the knowledge; stewards
    keep it trustworthy."
  uncertainty: >-
    The application of Ostrom's principles (developed for physical
    common-pool resources like fisheries and irrigation systems) to digital
    knowledge commons is an analogy the article asserts rather than
    empirically demonstrates for this specific context.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/source-systems.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
